✅ 1. SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)
Purpose: Sending emails from client to server and between servers
Port:
25 (default, often blocked by ISPs)
587 (with STARTTLS encryption)
465 (with SSL/TLS encryption)
Direction: Outgoing mail
Used by: Mail clients to send emails (e.g., Outlook → mail server)

✅ 2. IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol)
Purpose: Accessing emails stored on a remote mail server
Port:
143 (unencrypted)
993 (with SSL/TLS encryption)
Direction: Incoming mail
Behavior:
Keeps emails on the server
Syncs across multiple devices
Ideal for: Users who access mail from multiple devices

✅ 3. POP3 (Post Office Protocol version 3)
Purpose: Downloading emails from the server to local device
Port:
110 (unencrypted)
995 (with SSL/TLS encryption)
Direction: Incoming mail
Behavior:
Downloads and often deletes emails from server
Ideal for: Single-device access or backup scenarios

🧰 Email Clients (e.g., Microsoft Outlook)
Graphical tools used to send, receive, and manage email
Support for configuring accounts using:
SMTP (sending)
IMAP or POP3 (receiving)
Commonly used in:
Business environments (Outlook + Exchange)
Personal email via IMAP/SMTP (e.g., Gmail, custom domains)

